                <pb facs="0273" n="159" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg159"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R296/4284: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Edge</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-07-06T12:00:00+01:00">July 6, 1931—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received
                        July 6—8:02 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>410. Your 335, July 5, 10 p.m. A hasty reading of your criticisms of the French
                    proposal contained in our 405, July 5, 3 a.m., indicates difficulties on points
                    of drafting on the first seven paragraphs which I feel can be adjusted since
                    there is little question that it was the French intention to meet our points of
                    view on these subjects at the session at which this draft, which had been passed
                    by the Council of Ministers, was discussed and which did not close until 1
                    o’clock. We concentrated our entire attention on paragraph 7 concerning
                    deliveries in kind and warned that paragraph 8 would not in all probability be
                    acceptable since it was not germane to the subject. At that late hour there was
                    no time for discussion of the other paragraphs and we said we would seek your
                    reaction. Mr. Mellon and I are seeing Flandin at noon today.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edge</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
